        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Chesky said Airbnb offered distinctive, one-of-a-kind homes but less predictability than hotels, whose advantage was that guests knew what to expect. Guest Favorites were intended to pair Airbnb’s uniqueness with hotel-like reliability by highlighting homes highly rated by guests.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "The tradeoff",
            "content": "Airbnb’s homes were unique, but because each was one of a kind, guests could be uncertain whether the home would match what they booked—a “moment of truth” at check-in. Hotels were less special or unique, but guests knew what they were going to get. Chesky described reliability as Airbnb’s “Achilles heel.”",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "How Guest Favorites addressed it",
            "content": "Guest Favorites were meant to combine Airbnb’s distinctive homes with the reliability guests expected from hotels. Airbnb used 370 million reviews, millions of customer-service tickets, and host-cancellation data to identify its top two million homes; the collection was named Guest Favorites because those homes received the highest guest ratings.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1"
            ]
          }
