      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Chesky said Airbnb offered unique, one-of-a-kind homes but less predictability, while hotels were less special but reliably consistent. Guest Favorites were intended to bring hotel-like reliability to Airbnb’s distinctive stays.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "The tradeoff",
            "content": "Airbnb’s homes were unique, but guests could not always know what they would get until check-in—the “moment of truth.” Hotels were less special or unique, but guests knew what to expect. Chesky described reliability as Airbnb’s Achilles’ heel.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "How Guest Favorites addressed it",
            "content": "Guest Favorites were meant to combine Airbnb’s uniqueness with the reliability guests expect from a hotel. Airbnb selected two million homes that guests rated highest, using signals from 370 million reviews, millions of customer-service tickets, and host-cancellation data.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S3"
            ]
          }
        ],
